    <!-- ══ BENEFIT ROWS ══ -->
    <section class="of-benefits">
      <div class="of-benefits__container">
        <div class="of-benefits__header">
          <div class="of-benefits__eyebrow">The Benefits</div>
          <h2 class="of-benefits__headline">Four Ways Film Serves Historic and Active Congregations</h2>
          <p class="of-benefits__intro">Church window film for Philadelphia buildings addresses preservation, comfort, energy performance, and security from the existing glass outward.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/ch-uv.jpg" alt="Historic stained glass rose window protected by conservation-grade UV film on interior clear glass" title="Historic stained glass rose window protected by conservation-grade UV film on interior clear glass" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Historic Preservation</div><h3 class="of-row__title">UV Film That Protects What Makes Philadelphia's Churches Irreplaceable</h3><p class="of-row__body">Historic stained glass windows — the medieval-inspired rose windows, figural panels, and memorial glass of Philadelphia's 19th-century churches — are irreplaceable. UV radiation causes the organic dyes and associated historic materials to fade over decades, especially where sunlight strikes wood furnishings, textiles, and painted finishes.</p><p class="of-row__body">Conservation-grade UV window film is installed on interior clear glass panels, not on the stained glass itself. That distinction matters: church window film for Philadelphia preservation work provides 99% UV rejection without altering the visible light character of the stained glass. Learn more about <a href="/benefits/uv-blocking-window-film-philadelphia/">UV-blocking window film in Philadelphia</a>.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Film is applied to compatible clear panels, not historic art glass.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>99% UV rejection protects glass-adjacent finishes and furnishings.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Compatible with preservation guidance from the <a href="https://www.nps.gov/subjects/historicpreservation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Park Service</a>.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/ch-energy.jpg" alt="Historic Philadelphia church fellowship hall with Low-E energy-saving window film on tall windows" title="Historic Philadelphia church fellowship hall with Low-E energy-saving window film on tall windows" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Energy Savings</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Cut the Energy Bills That Drain Philadelphia Congregations' Resources</h3><p class="of-row__body">A historic church with 40-foot ceilings, single-pane windows, and stone walls loses heat in winter and gains heat in summer at rates that modern HVAC systems struggle to compensate for. Energy performance is not just a facilities issue; it affects what a congregation can spend on ministry, staffing, outreach, and building care.</p><p class="of-row__body">3M Thinsulate Climate Control window film improves both summer solar rejection and winter insulation through a Low-E coating — a dual benefit for buildings with poor baseline thermal performance. Many congregations see measurable comfort improvement in their first heating or cooling season. See <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/buildings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ENERGY STAR buildings guidance</a> and our <a href="/benefits/energy-savings/">energy savings window film</a> page.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Low-E film reflects interior heat back into the space during winter.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Solar control reduces heat gain in fellowship halls and sanctuary wings.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Product documentation helps facilities committees compare film options.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/ch-comfort.jpg" alt="Comfortable church congregation seating with glare-reduction film preserving warm natural sanctuary light" title="Comfortable church congregation seating with glare-reduction film preserving warm natural sanctuary light" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Congregation Comfort</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Services and Fellowship Without the Glare and Temperature Swings</h3><p class="of-row__body">Congregants sitting near windows during morning services often experience intense glare during summer months. Afternoon fellowship hall events can become uncomfortably hot, especially in rooms with large clear panes and limited shade.</p><p class="of-row__body">Glare reduction film cuts glare by up to 90% while maintaining the warmth and beauty of natural light, which is essential for worship spaces where light quality matters. Church window film for Philadelphia sanctuaries can also reduce temperature swings near windows, making services, meetings, and fellowship events easier to use year-round.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Reduces harsh light without closing blinds over meaningful windows.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Helps fellowship halls, classrooms, and offices feel more stable.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Preserves natural light quality for worship and community events.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image"><img src="/images/ch-security.jpg" alt="Church entry glass protected with security and anti-graffiti window film in Philadelphia" title="Church entry glass protected with security and anti-graffiti window film in Philadelphia" width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <div class="of-row__text"><div class="of-row__eyebrow">Security</div><h3 class="of-row__title">Protect Your Congregation and Your Building's Irreplaceable Glass</h3><p class="of-row__body">Churches face real security concerns: theft, vandalism, glass etching, and forced entry through doors or accessible windows. Anti-intrusion film on entry glass doors delays forced entry, buying time for response and reducing the chance that broken glass becomes an immediate hazard.</p><p class="of-row__body">Anti-graffiti film on accessible glass, metal, and mirrors prevents permanent etching damage because the sacrificial layer can be replaced. For churches that have experienced previous break-ins or vandalism, security film is a meaningful passive deterrent. See our <a href="/benefits/anti-graffiti/">anti-graffiti film</a> and <a href="/benefits/safety-and-security/">safety and security film</a> resources.</p><ul class="of-row__bullets"><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Protects glass, metal, and mirrors — not just windows.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Security film holds broken glass together after impact.</li><li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Anti-graffiti film protects street-level architectural glass.</li></ul></div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ FILM TYPES SLIDER ══ -->
    <section class="of-ftype" id="ofFilmTypes">
      <div class="of-ftype__track" id="ofFtypeTrack">
        <div class="of-ftype__slide of-ftype-active" data-slide="0"><img src="/images/ch-slider-uv.jpg" alt="UV conservation film for historic Philadelphia church stained glass protection" title="UV conservation film for historic Philadelphia church stained glass protection" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">01</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">UV Conservation Film.
Protect the Glass Behind the Glass.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Clear conservation-grade UV film protects stained glass, memorial windows, wood, textiles, and painted finishes while preserving the light character of the sanctuary.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Will UV film damage our historic stained glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>No. Conservation-grade UV film is installed on interior clear glass panels, the storm glass inside the original stained glass, not on the stained glass itself. The original glass is never touched.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does film affect the appearance of stained glass from outside?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Not at all when properly specified. The film goes on the interior clear protection panel, which is already visually secondary from outside. The stained glass appearance is completely unchanged.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What UV protection level does the film provide?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Our conservation-grade UV film blocks 99% of UV radiation, aligning with preservation standards for protecting historic materials.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Ask about UV conservation film →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="1"><img src="/images/ch-slider-energy.jpg" alt="Solar control film in a high-ceiling Philadelphia sanctuary for energy savings" title="Solar control film in a high-ceiling Philadelphia sanctuary for energy savings" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">02</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Solar Control Film.
Relief for High Ceilings.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Solar control and Low-E films help high-ceiling sanctuaries, parish halls, and classrooms manage heat gain in summer and heat loss in winter.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can film help a sanctuary with very high ceilings?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. The glass is often the most direct path for solar heat gain and winter heat loss. Film improves the window surface without changing the building envelope.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Why use Thinsulate for churches?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Thinsulate Climate Control film has a Low-E coating that provides both summer solar control and winter insulating benefit, which is valuable in older single-pane religious buildings.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How should committees think about payback?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>We avoid one-size-fits-all claims. We review glass type, exposure, HVAC use, and room schedule, then provide product performance data so your committee can evaluate likely impact.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Review energy film options →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="2"><img src="/images/ch-slider-comfort.jpg" alt="Glare reduction film improving congregation comfort in a church service" title="Glare reduction film improving congregation comfort in a church service" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">03</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Glare Reduction Film.
Comfort Without Losing Light.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Glare reduction film softens harsh sun during services, music rehearsals, classroom use, and fellowship hall events while preserving the warmth of natural light.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Will glare film make the sanctuary feel dark?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Not when the right visible light transmission is selected. We use samples to balance glare control with the light quality that belongs in a worship space.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it help morning and afternoon glare?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Film selection depends on orientation, so east-facing morning glare and west-facing afternoon glare may receive different recommendations.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can glare film help fellowship hall events?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Fellowship halls with large clear panes often benefit from glare and heat reduction, making meals, meetings, and youth programs more comfortable.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Improve congregation comfort →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="3"><img src="/images/ch-slider-security.jpg" alt="Security and anti-intrusion film on church entry glass doors" title="Security and anti-intrusion film on church entry glass doors" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">04</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Security &amp; Anti-Intrusion Film.
More Time When Glass Breaks.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Security film reinforces entry doors, sidelites, offices, and accessible windows by holding shattered glass together after impact.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does security film stop forced entry?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Security film does not make glass impenetrable, but it significantly delays entry by keeping broken glass bonded together, giving alarms and responders more time.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it protect people from broken glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Fragment retention is one of the core benefits of safety and security film, reducing the risk of loose shards after impact.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can it be installed on historic entry doors?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Often, yes, if the glass is compatible and access allows proper installation. We assess historic doors carefully and document the recommended film and attachment approach.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Assess entry glass →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="4"><img src="/images/ch-slider-graffiti.jpg" alt="Anti-graffiti film protecting street-level church architectural glass from etching" title="Anti-graffiti film protecting street-level church architectural glass from etching" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">05</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Anti-Graffiti Film.
Sacrificial Surface Protection.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Anti-graffiti film protects glass, metal, and mirrors from etching, scratching, and tagging, especially on street-level panels and accessible entry areas.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What surfaces can anti-graffiti film protect?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>It protects glass, metal, and mirrors — not just windows. We specify it for exterior-accessible glass, vestibules, restrooms, and polished interior surfaces.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What happens when the film is damaged?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>The damaged sacrificial layer is removed and replaced, while the protected surface beneath stays intact.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Is the protection visible?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>No. Anti-graffiti film is optically clear, so it protects architectural glass without changing the look of the building.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Protect vulnerable surfaces →</a></div></div>
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="5"><img src="/images/ch-slider-decorative.jpg" alt="Decorative frosted privacy film in church offices and fellowship hall partitions" title="Decorative frosted privacy film in church offices and fellowship hall partitions" class="of-ftype__bg" /><div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div><div class="of-ftype__content"><div class="of-ftype__step">06</div><h2 class="of-ftype__title">Decorative Film.
Privacy for Working Spaces.</h2><p class="of-ftype__desc">Decorative, frosted, and patterned film adds privacy in church offices, counseling rooms, classrooms, and fellowship hall partitions without permanent etched glass.</p><div class="of-ftype__faq"><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can frosted film protect privacy in church offices?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Frosted film obscures view lines while allowing light through, making it useful for offices, counseling rooms, nurseries, and administrative spaces.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can decorative film improve fellowship hall partitions?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Decorative gradients and patterns can make glass partitions feel intentional while improving privacy between activities.</div></div><div class="of-ftype__faq-item"><button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Are decorative films preservation-compatible?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>For landmark buildings, we focus decorative film on interior non-historic glass or reversible surfaces and provide product documentation when review is needed.</div></div></div><a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Explore decorative film →</a></div></div>
      </div>
